WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.296 --> 00:00:12.296
[MUSIC]

00:00:32.726 --> 00:00:36.430
Welcome to Leadership for
Society Reimagining Work Post-COVID.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:39.850
I'm Brian Lowery, a professor at
the Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:44.674
And today we're talking about the gig
economy with Sam Bright Chief product and

00:00:44.674 --> 00:00:46.622
Experience Officer at Upwork.

00:00:46.622 --> 00:00:49.490
And Melissa Valentine,
professor at Stanford University in

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.082
the Management Science and
Engineering Department and

00:00:52.082 --> 00:00:55.720
Co-director of the Center for
Work Technology and Organization.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.212
Thank you both.

00:00:57.212 --> 00:00:59.451
&gt;&gt; Thank you for having us.

00:00:59.451 --> 00:01:01.180
&gt;&gt; Great, so I'm going to jump right in.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:06.179
And I'm going to start with you, Melissa,
can you define the gig economy for us?

00:01:06.179 --> 00:01:07.067
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think so,

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:10.690
there are a lot of different labels
that are kind of relevant to that.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.800
But I like the definition
that Mary Gray uses,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.848
she's a researcher at Microsoft Research.

00:01:16.848 --> 00:01:21.624
And she talks about anytime that you're
using an API, it's like a software call,

00:01:21.624 --> 00:01:24.149
anytime you're using
an API to access labor

00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:26.966
then you're participating
in the gig economy.

00:01:26.966 --> 00:01:30.885
So labor access through an API,
that's how I would define the gig economy.

00:01:30.885 --> 00:01:34.295
&gt;&gt; Okay, so labor access through
a certain type of technology, you think?

00:01:34.295 --> 00:01:36.705
That's what defines the gig economy,
great.

00:01:36.705 --> 00:01:40.615
So Sam, can you tell us about Upwork and
how it intersects with the gig economy?

00:01:41.825 --> 00:01:45.756
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so first of all,
thanks for having us.

00:01:45.756 --> 00:01:50.542
I think about Upwork, we're a work
marketplace that connects businesses and

00:01:50.542 --> 00:01:52.873
highly skilled independent talent.

00:01:52.873 --> 00:01:58.571
Many of whom are entrepreneurs or small
business owners who are doing work on

00:01:58.571 --> 00:02:04.908
behalf of individuals like you and I, or
all the way up to Fortune 100 companies.

00:02:04.908 --> 00:02:06.844
And we provide a solution for

00:02:06.844 --> 00:02:11.930
them to build relationships
between clients and freelancers.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.205
We tend the view is much
more relational and

00:02:14.205 --> 00:02:17.184
project oriented than
transactional relationships.

00:02:17.184 --> 00:02:22.804
Or interactions that are often
the associated with a gig economy

00:02:22.804 --> 00:02:29.271
such as taking an Uber ride or having
a handyman or handy person mount a TV.

00:02:29.271 --> 00:02:34.456
So from my perspective, anything I think
Upwork and the gig economy while separate,

00:02:34.456 --> 00:02:38.716
both fit under this, like broader
umbrella of flexible work models.

00:02:38.716 --> 00:02:43.650
And so part of the differentiator is that
the jobs on our platform, have great pay.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.140
They have high quality work because
it's mission critical to businesses.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:52.125
So we serve everyone from like I mentioned
from one person startups to 30% of

00:02:52.125 --> 00:02:56.669
the Fortune 100 are our clients
including the Airbnb and world etc.

00:02:56.669 --> 00:03:01.334
And we find the clients use
our platform to connect

00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:06.347
with different freelancers
who are highly skilled.

00:03:06.347 --> 00:03:10.866
Could be developers that could be
engineering freelancers to proactively

00:03:10.866 --> 00:03:13.380
execute major business initiatives.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.300
So I see them as adjacent but separate.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.930
&gt;&gt; Got it and how do you expect
Upwork to evolve moving forward?

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:24.090
So the pandemic has happened,
work is shifting in all sorts of ways.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.768
What do you think is going to
happen in terms of Upwork and

00:03:27.768 --> 00:03:30.070
its evolution in this new space.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:34.502
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, I think if I take a step
back from Upwork I think it's just taking

00:03:34.502 --> 00:03:38.331
a look at what's going on in terms
of society when it comes to work.

00:03:38.331 --> 00:03:41.990
I think work as a construct
has been disrupted.

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:46.804
We went through this year and
a half experiment where many folks who had

00:03:46.804 --> 00:03:51.292
traditionally believed that work
can only be done a certain way,

00:03:51.292 --> 00:03:55.570
tied to certain previous
preconceptions of work.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:59.370
Everyone was sheltering in place in
many cases and the knowledge economy.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:03.270
And managers had to adapt
workers have to adapt.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:07.668
And I think it led to sort of a change and
a re-evaluation in terms

00:04:07.668 --> 00:04:11.266
of the perceptions on what
does work actually mean.

00:04:11.266 --> 00:04:15.468
Does it need to be connected to a badge,
does it need to be connected to a work

00:04:15.468 --> 00:04:18.498
location, does it need to
be connected to a commute.

00:04:18.498 --> 00:04:22.972
So if we think about that fundamental
reexamination of work within society.

00:04:22.972 --> 00:04:24.744
Then you think about as the backdrop.

00:04:24.744 --> 00:04:29.535
Then you think about what Upwork
is providing in terms of being

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:34.509
a place where working get done no
matter where you are no matter,

00:04:34.509 --> 00:04:39.223
we have over 10,000 skills so
multiple represented.

00:04:39.223 --> 00:04:42.215
So no matter what
particular skill you have,

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:46.399
that gives us a lot of opportunity
to solve customer problems.

00:04:46.399 --> 00:04:50.559
And to provide the control and flexibility
that freelancers are looking for

00:04:50.559 --> 00:04:53.770
in terms of being able to
work however they want to.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:57.925
So I see as work continues to evolve
Upwork being at the forefront of that.

00:04:57.925 --> 00:05:02.803
And part of what we've been doing over the
past year as we transition from a single

00:05:02.803 --> 00:05:07.111
product line company to a multi product
line company is really enabling

00:05:07.111 --> 00:05:11.514
freelancers and clients to be able to
get work done however they see fit.

00:05:11.514 --> 00:05:15.170
As changing customer
expectations are evolving.

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:21.180
Our aspiration and
our charge is to really lead that charge.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.340
And to, fulfill our vision of being
the place where the world wants to work.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.090
&gt;&gt; That's, there's a lot going on there.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:31.910
So I'm going to come back, and
ask some questions about that.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.346
But let me turn to you for
a second, Melissa.

00:05:34.346 --> 00:05:37.157
I know you've done research
in the gig economy.

00:05:37.157 --> 00:05:38.867
How do you see the gig economy now and

00:05:38.867 --> 00:05:41.490
where do you see it going as
a function of the pandemic?

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:43.622
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean,

00:05:43.622 --> 00:05:46.460
I think there's a lot that connects
in with what Sam was just saying.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:51.066
I think that the pandemic,
culturally made a lot more people

00:05:51.066 --> 00:05:54.691
aware of the possibility
of working remotely.

00:05:54.691 --> 00:05:58.354
Something that's characteristic
of work done on Upwork and

00:05:58.354 --> 00:06:02.870
other platforms like Upwork, is that
a lot of it has been done remotely.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:06.735
So this is a way of life, it's a way
of working that's more common and

00:06:06.735 --> 00:06:10.815
that's more experienced for
people who work on platforms like Upwork.

00:06:10.815 --> 00:06:15.362
So now that more companies have
experience with remote work and

00:06:15.362 --> 00:06:20.958
can kind of see what it's like and how
much they can get done not in the office.

00:06:20.958 --> 00:06:25.835
It I think that it sort of connects
like larger kind of older like

00:06:25.835 --> 00:06:30.713
bureaucratic organizations with
the kind of work that's done

00:06:30.713 --> 00:06:33.856
on platform based company like Upwork.

00:06:33.856 --> 00:06:35.316
I think that the difference and

00:06:35.316 --> 00:06:38.900
this is something I would love
to ask Sam about at some point.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:43.999
I think one thing that I think
where there's kind of a middle area

00:06:43.999 --> 00:06:49.540
that hasn't been connected yet
is between large companies.

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:52.850
That have a lot of team based work or
a lot of interdependent work.

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:56.750
We have a lot of complex projects that
involve a lot of different functions and

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:00.350
a lot of different kind of expertise
that's brought together in larger

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:01.850
projects.

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:04.842
So Upwork traditionally
has been more of a market,

00:07:04.842 --> 00:07:08.808
it's more like even though they're
very high skilled freelancers

00:07:08.808 --> 00:07:13.390
is matching sort of client to
freelancer in more of a market design.

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:17.908
My understanding is that Upwork is
moving into more team based projects and

00:07:17.908 --> 00:07:20.460
offering more project based work.

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:24.270
But I think that's going to be something
that people who are interested in the gig

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:26.410
economy are going to be watching for.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:28.405
Cause there's so much work, there's so

00:07:28.405 --> 00:07:32.281
into high value work that's kind of right
there in between market production and

00:07:32.281 --> 00:07:34.675
organizational production
if that makes sense.

00:07:34.675 --> 00:07:39.127
And I guess I'm seeing with the pandemic
I guess I'm seeing that gap that sort of

00:07:39.127 --> 00:07:40.930
space between closing faster.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:45.373
&gt;&gt; So I'll let Sam respond to
that question in a second but

00:07:45.373 --> 00:07:49.237
tell me, why should I well I'm
like remote work so it's fine and

00:07:49.237 --> 00:07:52.549
it's also kind of easy given
what I do most of the time.

00:07:52.549 --> 00:07:53.966
I have to teach them in person but

00:07:53.966 --> 00:07:57.900
otherwise like when I do research I
can be wherever so It works for me.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:02.350
But tell me why I shouldn't be worried
about the tyranny of remote work.

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:04.560
So all the language is like,
it's going to be great.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.990
There's flexibility but
in that world, there is choice.

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:13.977
But let's imagine that organizations
decide it's cheaper to have remote work,

00:08:13.977 --> 00:08:18.900
but I or someone like me or other people
aren't really excited about it, right?

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:22.901
So tell me why I shouldn't be worried
about this shift that happened?

00:08:22.901 --> 00:08:27.956
Or better yet, what should I be worried
about when we think about this shift?

00:08:29.618 --> 00:08:32.589
&gt;&gt; So
I think it's such a great question, and

00:08:32.589 --> 00:08:38.010
this is what I'm seeing a lot of
organizations grapple with right now.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:40.467
You end up with the remote
work enthusiasts and

00:08:40.467 --> 00:08:44.820
then the remote work skeptics and somehow
they have to find a way to work together.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:49.566
I think what we're seeing is compromise
doesn't really work because it

00:08:49.566 --> 00:08:53.650
sort of reproduces what's
hard about both systems.

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:58.905
So if you have a company that has
a culture of more remote work skeptics,

00:08:58.905 --> 00:09:03.897
and you end up going more remote and
to make more remote work, work,

00:09:03.897 --> 00:09:06.897
you have to do a lot more documentation.

00:09:06.897 --> 00:09:10.820
A lot more asynchronous coordination,
and there's a lot of cost to that.

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:14.390
So you really have to invest
heavily into that kind of work.

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:18.128
Alternatively, if you have people who
want a little bit of the flexibility but

00:09:18.128 --> 00:09:21.863
are relying much more on real time
collaboration, then there's a whole other

00:09:21.863 --> 00:09:26.020
set of coordination costs to get people
into the office at the same time.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.780
So I don't know that I've seen this

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:32.840
kind of solved in a way that
really combines the two.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.714
I'm seeing more sort of like sorting,
people are sort of sorting into,

00:09:36.714 --> 00:09:40.541
self selecting into companies that
are solving it one way or the other.

00:09:42.531 --> 00:09:44.299
&gt;&gt; And Sam, what do you think about that?

00:09:44.299 --> 00:09:49.405
So I know that you have a wide range
of people who are on your platform,

00:09:49.405 --> 00:09:52.060
so it's not just low wage workers.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:55.690
There are people who are technically
very savvy and skilled, but

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:58.930
how should we think about how
far this is going to go, right?

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:03.170
So would you have your job, would you
do your job via this form of work?

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:09.366
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think the,
Think a couple things.

00:10:09.366 --> 00:10:15.594
I mean, first of all, there are many folks
who probably never considered remote work,

00:10:15.594 --> 00:10:20.393
who during the past year and
a half realized that there were benefits

00:10:20.393 --> 00:10:24.938
associated with being able to not
have to deal with the commute or

00:10:24.938 --> 00:10:27.991
be able to collaborate in different ways.

00:10:27.991 --> 00:10:32.088
For me personally,
I have an 18 month old son, and

00:10:32.088 --> 00:10:35.760
my son was born at
the height of the pandemic.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:39.889
And the ability to not be
in a commute every day and

00:10:39.889 --> 00:10:44.424
actually be there for
him while he's getting ready or

00:10:44.424 --> 00:10:50.590
to just do our night-time routines
is transformative in many regards.

00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:55.586
So I do think you have these folks
who are, especially different

00:10:55.586 --> 00:11:01.043
generations who are coming to work for
the first time during the moment

00:11:01.043 --> 00:11:06.432
where remote work is more of an option,
who are more remote natives.

00:11:06.432 --> 00:11:11.858
And so just like digital natives or folks
who are very comfortable with digital

00:11:11.858 --> 00:11:16.805
first organizations, I think we're
going to have more remote natives.

00:11:16.805 --> 00:11:22.165
I also joined Upwork during a moment
were everything was done remotely.

00:11:22.165 --> 00:11:26.940
So I did the entire interview
process I joined Upwork in November,

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:30.360
I did the entire interview
process virtually.

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:35.380
I hadn't met anyone on the leadership
team or on my own staff.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:39.461
I was sent out a laptop and
was like go off and do things.

00:11:39.461 --> 00:11:43.761
And so it was only subsequently,
probably six months in or so,

00:11:43.761 --> 00:11:49.118
when the pandemic started to get a bit
better and folks had gotten vaccinated,

00:11:49.118 --> 00:11:53.907
and were able to rapidly test that I
was able to go and meet with some of my

00:11:53.907 --> 00:11:58.896
colleagues, and meet my boss, and
meet other folks who were on my team.

00:11:58.896 --> 00:12:03.086
And it was kind of a unique dynamic,
probably the only time in my life thus far

00:12:03.086 --> 00:12:06.570
where people who you've never met
run up and give you a big hug.

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:10.848
because they've been collaborating
with you for many years or not for

00:12:10.848 --> 00:12:14.060
many years, but for many months.

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:18.950
So that's a really interesting dynamic,
I think that's at play as well.

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:22.395
I think that remote work's
going to continue to evolve,

00:12:22.395 --> 00:12:24.709
and that we're going to keep adapting.

00:12:24.709 --> 00:12:28.512
You think about all the different
collaborative tools that have emerged, and

00:12:28.512 --> 00:12:31.968
that continue to emerge as people
are learning how to get work done, and

00:12:31.968 --> 00:12:34.190
how to triage during these moments.

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:38.041
And I also think that there's
various spaces on the continuum for

00:12:38.041 --> 00:12:39.309
companies, right?

00:12:39.309 --> 00:12:43.160
Like, for Upwork,
we're choosing to be remote first.

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:47.452
So when our offices in San Francisco,
in Chicago, open next month,

00:12:47.452 --> 00:12:50.782
we're still going to have
those physical offices, and

00:12:50.782 --> 00:12:55.780
we're going to give people options to
go into those offices as they see fit.

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:56.675
But it's not going to be required.

00:12:56.675 --> 00:13:00.559
I'll probably go into the office
every few weeks or so.

00:13:00.559 --> 00:13:05.515
And it'll create an opportunity for those
who are looking to collaborate in person

00:13:05.515 --> 00:13:08.294
or have those sync points
to be able to engage.

00:13:08.294 --> 00:13:12.199
But the one point I want to call out
though is that when we talk about remote

00:13:12.199 --> 00:13:16.140
work, some of it is really
predicated around remote from what?

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:19.299
because people have been
working in satellite offices,

00:13:19.299 --> 00:13:21.610
have been remote for the entire time.

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:25.186
It's just that up until now,
different leaders have gone and

00:13:25.186 --> 00:13:29.399
met with them or they've been able
to fly back to company headquarters.

00:13:29.399 --> 00:13:32.706
I think certainly,
geographically distributed companies or

00:13:32.706 --> 00:13:36.882
SMBs who have different locations have
always had folks who have been, quote,

00:13:36.882 --> 00:13:38.955
unquote, remote from headquarters.

00:13:38.955 --> 00:13:43.652
Just now, the question is how much do
you have to be proximate to an office

00:13:43.652 --> 00:13:48.503
versus you can work from home as your
office, and still be able to engage and

00:13:48.503 --> 00:13:51.200
connect with co-workers along the way?

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:55.669
&gt;&gt; And also, I want a draw distinction,
right, between just pure remote work and

00:13:55.669 --> 00:13:56.279
gig work.

00:13:56.279 --> 00:13:59.120
And gig,
we can also use the term freelance, right?

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:04.160
Because there's a flexibility, that's
certainly associated with remote, but

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:08.915
there's also flexibility and challenges
associated with being freelance.

00:14:08.915 --> 00:14:13.632
So how do you think about the cost and
benefits of the freelance work where

00:14:13.632 --> 00:14:18.349
they're not really connected to
the employer in the same kind of ongoing

00:14:18.349 --> 00:14:22.005
contractual relationship
that a regular employee is?

00:14:22.005 --> 00:14:25.310
How should we think about that
from your perspective, Sam?

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:30.816
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think we've
seen an increase in folks who

00:14:30.816 --> 00:14:35.377
are interested in freelancing
as part of the pandemic.

00:14:35.377 --> 00:14:41.845
I think that we did some research
around a freelance Ford Report.

00:14:41.845 --> 00:14:46.607
We found that 12% of the US workforce
began freelancing during the pandemic for

00:14:46.607 --> 00:14:48.170
the first time.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:52.314
Nearly half of Gen Z professionals
are freelancing in some capacity.

00:14:52.314 --> 00:14:55.759
And about three quarters of the folks
who have freelanced said they

00:14:55.759 --> 00:14:56.811
wouldn't go back.

00:14:56.811 --> 00:14:58.561
You couldn't pay them enough money.

00:14:58.561 --> 00:15:01.827
They love the control,
on average they have or

00:15:01.827 --> 00:15:07.358
the median number of clients that a
freelancer has according to freelancing in

00:15:07.358 --> 00:15:12.998
America report that was done in 2020
over a six month period is six clients.

00:15:12.998 --> 00:15:14.622
So a lot of the freelancers, you're right,

00:15:14.622 --> 00:15:16.474
there's a delta between freelancing and
remote.

00:15:16.474 --> 00:15:19.499
Though obviously when folks are remote,
I think they start to

00:15:19.499 --> 00:15:23.760
question other assumptions about work and
are like, well, why not try freelancing?

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:28.711
Why not have the flexibility of being full
time at some points, being full time and

00:15:28.711 --> 00:15:31.727
moonlighting, being
freelance at some points,

00:15:31.727 --> 00:15:35.860
and just kind of evolving
their careers as they see fit?

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:41.129
I think you do see more and
more individuals willing to try out

00:15:41.129 --> 00:15:46.308
this mode of work because of
the control that it gives them.

00:15:46.308 --> 00:15:48.624
They don't have to put
their eggs in one basket.

00:15:48.624 --> 00:15:50.846
They can upskill at their own pace.

00:15:50.846 --> 00:15:54.000
They can work for multiple companies, etc.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:55.441
As they go along.

00:15:55.441 --> 00:15:58.382
And freelancing doesn't have
to be something that someone

00:15:58.382 --> 00:15:59.567
chooses permanently.

00:15:59.567 --> 00:16:03.038
Like there are people who may
decide to freelance for a time and

00:16:03.038 --> 00:16:05.190
then become a full time worker again.

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:08.959
And then another time land
to freelancing again,

00:16:08.959 --> 00:16:12.558
its just another option for
folks to consider.

00:16:12.558 --> 00:16:13.419
&gt;&gt; And Melissa,

00:16:13.419 --> 00:16:18.394
our economy is built on the assumption of
full time work with an employer, right?

00:16:18.394 --> 00:16:22.386
Not just our economy, but the way our
lives are set up socially, right?

00:16:22.386 --> 00:16:25.378
So healthcare and other benefits.

00:16:25.378 --> 00:16:28.444
When you think about this
move toward freelance,

00:16:28.444 --> 00:16:32.970
what are the concerns you have or what
kind of problems do you see arising and

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:35.685
what would we need to
do to make that viable?

00:16:35.685 --> 00:16:38.053
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I appreciate the distinction
that you're making there.

00:16:38.053 --> 00:16:43.045
I think that the move to remote has
remote top of mind for a lot of us.

00:16:43.045 --> 00:16:46.534
But I think the questions the really
important and profound questions with

00:16:46.534 --> 00:16:49.411
the gig economy have to do with
the employment relationship,

00:16:49.411 --> 00:16:51.230
just like you're saying.

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:54.216
So previously, we have a structured so

00:16:54.216 --> 00:16:59.810
many of our benefits around full
time employment in organizations.

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:03.287
And the United States is a little bit
weird in that regard, we have health

00:17:03.287 --> 00:17:07.790
insurance and retired so many things are
structured around full time employment.

00:17:07.790 --> 00:17:13.382
So now with I sort of harken
back to what I was saying

00:17:13.382 --> 00:17:19.251
at the start with the API as
a software call to labor.

00:17:19.251 --> 00:17:22.550
The reason that I want to point to that
because this is bigger than Upwork.

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:28.331
Upwork is kind of amazing for
how long they've been revenue positive for

00:17:28.331 --> 00:17:32.195
how long and for
kind of how big they've gotten.

00:17:32.195 --> 00:17:37.373
But this gig economies is so
much bigger especially now with API's

00:17:37.373 --> 00:17:42.829
sort of connecting into lots of other
data systems that have like large

00:17:42.829 --> 00:17:48.298
remote workforces, you can like have
an API call to a pool of doctors.

00:17:48.298 --> 00:17:49.995
So Upwork has a lot of software.

00:17:49.995 --> 00:17:54.972
They have so many different kinds of
experts, but you could have it to doctors,

00:17:54.972 --> 00:17:56.917
to lawyers, to accountants.

00:17:56.917 --> 00:18:00.815
I met some MBAs who graduated
from Harvard business school.

00:18:00.815 --> 00:18:03.640
And now they have like, you can do
an API to get like a business plan.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:08.552
So there are so many different kind of
labor pools where you can have this call

00:18:08.552 --> 00:18:12.721
to the large Labor Code sort of
like source of project on demand.

00:18:12.721 --> 00:18:17.558
So it's that kind of on demand sourcing of
high value work that is kind of driving

00:18:17.558 --> 00:18:22.920
the conversation now about the employment
relationship that we're seeing.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:26.693
So I've interacted with a bunch of
different companies that have set up these

00:18:26.693 --> 00:18:29.350
models where you have
some sort of platform.

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:35.057
And then some sort of large labor
force and those freelancers are now,

00:18:35.057 --> 00:18:40.870
they had like the way that their
career trajectory looks is different.

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:42.368
The way that their training
once is different,

00:18:42.368 --> 00:18:44.380
the way that their social
group looks is different.

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:47.805
So there's so many differences and
how this kind of platform and

00:18:47.805 --> 00:18:51.113
network economy looks compared
what we've had in the past.

00:18:51.113 --> 00:18:52.835
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:18:52.835 --> 00:18:54.264
I'm sorry, I didn't mean to interrupt.

00:18:54.264 --> 00:18:58.028
&gt;&gt; I was just going to agree with you that
I think that the social safety net that

00:18:58.028 --> 00:19:01.991
has formed around full time employment
is what people are concerned about.

00:19:01.991 --> 00:19:05.041
&gt;&gt; And speaking of that, I want to
ask about your thoughts on prop 22.

00:19:05.041 --> 00:19:09.247
If you could explain a little
bit about what that was and

00:19:09.247 --> 00:19:12.643
how it played out that'd be really useful.

00:19:12.643 --> 00:19:17.536
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I want to actually just be
clear that I'm not a policy expert,

00:19:17.536 --> 00:19:19.412
like a lot of my research and

00:19:19.412 --> 00:19:24.986
expertise is on the structuring of work
in the gig economy and in organizations.

00:19:24.986 --> 00:19:30.853
So I'm hesitant to sort of speak about
the policy implications of these.

00:19:30.853 --> 00:19:33.865
I think the lesson that we
can kind of take from it and

00:19:33.865 --> 00:19:36.877
sort of what to walk away
from that question with,

00:19:36.877 --> 00:19:41.991
is that the employment classification that
we've used in the past doesn't apply.

00:19:41.991 --> 00:19:46.424
It's very complicated to apply
the employment classification that we've

00:19:46.424 --> 00:19:49.202
used for
decades to what's going on right now.

00:19:49.202 --> 00:19:53.693
Let me just give one small example that's
actually not related to Upwork or Uber,

00:19:53.693 --> 00:19:57.030
but I think really highlights
what's sort of at stake here.

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:00.410
So this goes again to
Mary Gray's research.

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:02.980
She's written a book called Ghost Work.

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:07.877
She looks at Amazon Mechanical Turk
which is another platform that sort

00:20:07.877 --> 00:20:10.172
of sources to a large labor pool.

00:20:10.172 --> 00:20:13.997
And that labor pool is doing
a lot of the work that trains

00:20:13.997 --> 00:20:18.665
the datasets that large corporations
are using to train their AI.

00:20:18.665 --> 00:20:24.065
So AI products sort of depend on
the Mechanical Turk workforce.

00:20:24.065 --> 00:20:30.393
That workforce is less seen and
less visible than Uber or Upwork.

00:20:30.393 --> 00:20:34.846
But they have sort of the same issues
of not necessarily having the safety of

00:20:34.846 --> 00:20:36.860
full time employment.

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:42.148
So Mechanical Turk workers might come
onto the platform and work for a day.

00:20:42.148 --> 00:20:46.631
And they'll do like they'll label images,
for example, to train a dataset.

00:20:46.631 --> 00:20:48.607
So we don't have a classification.

00:20:48.607 --> 00:20:51.879
We don't have a way of understanding
that kind of employment in our country

00:20:51.879 --> 00:20:52.416
right now.

00:20:52.416 --> 00:20:55.194
So we don't have a way to
protect those workers.

00:20:55.194 --> 00:20:58.352
So what Mary Gray suggests,
and I think this is right, and

00:20:58.352 --> 00:21:02.671
I think that prop 22 sort of relates to
this, we need to rethink our employment

00:21:02.671 --> 00:21:06.362
classification system and
I'm really much more fundamental way.

00:21:06.362 --> 00:21:07.919
&gt;&gt; And what does that look like?

00:21:07.919 --> 00:21:09.349
So we're reimagining.

00:21:09.349 --> 00:21:11.455
I mean, I won't obviously hold you to it.

00:21:11.455 --> 00:21:12.697
But what is that?

00:21:12.697 --> 00:21:14.516
What are we trying to accomplish?

00:21:14.516 --> 00:21:15.073
What's the goal?

00:21:15.073 --> 00:21:20.112
And how would you suggest where would
we start even thinking about it?

00:21:20.112 --> 00:21:23.632
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I really appreciate
her remarks on this and

00:21:23.632 --> 00:21:27.410
I can share some of the links
to her writing on this.

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:31.782
But she sort of argues that what we
need first is a cultural change,

00:21:31.782 --> 00:21:34.438
a cultural change where we recognize and

00:21:34.438 --> 00:21:39.534
make visible the kind of labor that is
powering the digital economy right now.

00:21:39.534 --> 00:21:44.592
A lot of those products sort of depend on
the invisibility of this kind of labor.

00:21:44.592 --> 00:21:46.775
So the more that we make visible and

00:21:46.775 --> 00:21:51.598
kind of value the contributions of all
the people who are working then the more

00:21:51.598 --> 00:21:55.689
that we understand the kind of
protections that they might need.

00:21:55.689 --> 00:21:59.982
To tie it to what I was saying about
employment classification it's really hard

00:21:59.982 --> 00:22:04.018
to say if these are self employed people
part time employed people full time

00:22:04.018 --> 00:22:08.185
employed people freelancers entrepreneurs
it sort of defies our historical

00:22:08.185 --> 00:22:09.934
understanding of employment.

00:22:09.934 --> 00:22:12.724
So, her argument is we
need a culture change.

00:22:12.724 --> 00:22:15.378
She makes the analogy to child labor laws,

00:22:15.378 --> 00:22:19.517
at some point society was well
that's not something we want to do.

00:22:19.517 --> 00:22:21.794
[LAUGH] We don't want to
have child labor laws.

00:22:21.794 --> 00:22:22.548
So child labor.

00:22:22.548 --> 00:22:27.310
So she suggests that we first need
a cultural change where we are much better

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:31.705
at valuing all of the work that
contributes to the economy right now.

00:22:31.705 --> 00:22:36.459
And then based on that, I

00:22:36.459 --> 00:22:41.631
don't know if I said you're not going to
hold me to this her argument is that.

00:22:41.631 --> 00:22:44.702
So I think one thing that we
as a society are thinking

00:22:44.702 --> 00:22:48.570
about is where the benefits come from and
how they get attached.

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:49.515
Is it from companies?

00:22:49.515 --> 00:22:51.484
Is it from the government
things like that.

00:22:51.484 --> 00:22:54.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah, because I guess one of the
questions I have is who's benefiting right

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:56.033
now from this shift, right?

00:22:56.033 --> 00:23:00.460
Is the benefit being shared equally
across different constituents.

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:03.250
So consumers,
people who are doing the work and

00:23:03.250 --> 00:23:07.510
then the people who are managing or
running the organizations.

00:23:07.510 --> 00:23:12.176
So do we think your from your perspective
using those the benefits that we're

00:23:12.176 --> 00:23:16.559
generating are being distributed
equally across those those different

00:23:16.559 --> 00:23:19.789
categories
&gt;&gt; I think that's a really insightful

00:23:19.789 --> 00:23:22.956
point, I think that's a really good point.

00:23:22.956 --> 00:23:25.200
I mean, I would be going off intuition.

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:28.116
I feel like it's an empirical
question that's really important, and

00:23:28.116 --> 00:23:29.530
worth kind of looking into.

00:23:29.530 --> 00:23:32.946
My understanding of where
value accrues right now,

00:23:32.946 --> 00:23:36.800
in a very digital economy,
as sort of owners of data.

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:38.170
Get a lot of value.

00:23:38.170 --> 00:23:41.634
So the workers are tagging data and
kind of creating datasets that are then

00:23:41.634 --> 00:23:44.894
owned by companies and kind of
producing really valuable data sets.

00:23:44.894 --> 00:23:49.835
So I would conjecture that a lot
of the value follows the data.

00:23:49.835 --> 00:23:53.510
&gt;&gt; And I'm curious about your
perspective on this in terms of

00:23:53.510 --> 00:23:57.960
I'll just focus on the power of
the employees or I guess the workers.

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:01.920
How do you see this way of working
in the freelance and that goes for

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:07.036
Upwork is mostly freelance not necessarily
gig and when we think about Uber or Lyft.

00:24:07.036 --> 00:24:12.380
But how is this freelance style of
work affecting worker power right

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:17.550
in their organizations and
the organizations they engage with?

00:24:19.230 --> 00:24:21.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think it's a great question.

00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:26.880
I think it's redefining access for work.

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:31.415
I think we're seeing companies change
their mind from talent acquisition, which

00:24:31.415 --> 00:24:35.752
presumes that you own work or own workers
to town access, which really empowers

00:24:35.752 --> 00:24:39.980
workers to not just work with you but
work with multiple companies as needed.

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:44.912
So I actually think that it's a pretty
important shift in terms of providing

00:24:44.912 --> 00:24:46.310
more access to that.

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:50.525
I think has the potential to be much more
equitable than prior modes of working.

00:24:50.525 --> 00:24:53.638
If you're not dependent
on the old boys network,

00:24:53.638 --> 00:24:58.533
if you're not dependent on being two
hours away from a company headquarters,

00:24:58.533 --> 00:25:03.351
if you're not dependent on who you know,
or a resume in some cases in order for

00:25:03.351 --> 00:25:08.053
you to be able to work and provide for
your family and provide for yourself.

00:25:08.053 --> 00:25:12.744
That creates a lot more opportunities
I think for workers to not only get

00:25:12.744 --> 00:25:17.279
things done, but build their careers
in ways that suit their lives in

00:25:17.279 --> 00:25:22.280
a very different way and be able to
work to live as opposed to live to work.

00:25:22.280 --> 00:25:28.560
So I can't claim to be a policy expert or
speak on those plants.

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:33.072
But I can certainly say from
a freelancing perspective,

00:25:33.072 --> 00:25:36.926
especially for
highly skilled knowledge work,

00:25:36.926 --> 00:25:42.002
like what we found is that the many
other freelancers choose to,

00:25:42.002 --> 00:25:46.796
like the choice that they're
afforded through being able to

00:25:46.796 --> 00:25:50.192
participate in a freelancing lifestyle.

00:25:50.192 --> 00:25:55.242
And in some cases, they get access
to companies that probably wouldn't

00:25:55.242 --> 00:26:00.376
have considered them previously or
might not have brought them in through

00:26:00.376 --> 00:26:05.461
being able to work with those companies
through a platform such as ours.

00:26:05.461 --> 00:26:10.923
An interesting stat too,
we were seeing how it impacts

00:26:10.923 --> 00:26:17.470
education as well because we
survey freelancers periodically.

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:22.461
And even though 81% of them say their
college education is useful to the work

00:26:22.461 --> 00:26:27.449
they do now, of those with a four year
college degree, about two thirds of them

00:26:27.449 --> 00:26:31.559
said that they wish they had
instead obtained a two year degree.

00:26:31.559 --> 00:26:34.467
And supplemented their education
with online training so

00:26:34.467 --> 00:26:38.513
they could focus on upskilling on the
things that were most interesting to them.

00:26:38.513 --> 00:26:41.488
And so it's just a paradigm,
I think a paradigm shift,

00:26:41.488 --> 00:26:44.690
I think the social contract
is being rewritten.

00:26:44.690 --> 00:26:49.820
And it's giving more power to workers
to decide who and where and how

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:56.130
many companies they want to work with in
ways that we haven't experienced before.

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:57.740
So I think that's pretty exciting.

00:26:57.740 --> 00:27:02.340
And probably has the DNI implications
as well, in many real ways.

00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:06.466
&gt;&gt; And companies are still pretty powerful
though so I wonder what kind of safeguards

00:27:06.466 --> 00:27:09.539
you build in for the people that
do freelance work at Upwork.

00:27:09.539 --> 00:27:12.108
How do they make sure their
interests are protected

00:27:12.108 --> 00:27:14.385
when they are working
with an organization?

00:27:14.385 --> 00:27:20.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so, companies, so
freelancers can apply to jobs.

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:22.350
There's multiple ways to work, right.

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:27.560
Freelancers can apply to jobs that
clients have posted on the platform.

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:33.108
They can actually be invited to jobs that
clients that posted on the platform,

00:27:33.108 --> 00:27:36.972
and they can get,
they can set their own hourly rates,

00:27:36.972 --> 00:27:39.677
how much they're willing to charge.

00:27:39.677 --> 00:27:46.765
And fixed project costs can kind of
determine what companies are willing

00:27:46.765 --> 00:27:52.960
to to hire them for
the skills that they are producing.

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:57.880
We also have project catalog,
which gives more project oriented work.

00:27:57.880 --> 00:28:03.503
So freelancers can actually kind of have
this browse and buy upfront pricing,

00:28:03.503 --> 00:28:08.802
say I'm going to design a logo for
X amount of dollars and get two revisions.

00:28:08.802 --> 00:28:10.683
And so while they're sleeping,

00:28:10.683 --> 00:28:14.788
someone can hire them to do that work
based on browsing their portfolio.

00:28:14.788 --> 00:28:19.383
And then we also have talent scout which
actually provides vetting of clients as

00:28:19.383 --> 00:28:23.635
well as vetting of talent, so that
talent get that flexibility in terms of

00:28:23.635 --> 00:28:26.880
understanding the clients
that they're working for.

00:28:26.880 --> 00:28:28.988
So we have a variety of trust and

00:28:28.988 --> 00:28:34.679
safety features that we have put in place
to protect both sides in the marketplace.

00:28:34.679 --> 00:28:39.454
Talent to make sure they're working with
good clients, and then clients to make

00:28:39.454 --> 00:28:44.750
sure they're working with great talent,
and everyone's learning as they go.

00:28:44.750 --> 00:28:49.310
So I think it's really important that both
sides of the marketplace are invested in.

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:53.563
And the flexibility for talent to be
able to work however much they want,

00:28:53.563 --> 00:28:55.654
with whoever they want to work for,

00:28:55.654 --> 00:29:00.047
for however long the duration of time
that they want to work with them is truly

00:29:00.047 --> 00:29:04.047
unique when you think about work
as we traditionally thought of it.

00:29:04.047 --> 00:29:09.353
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and if I'm someone who's
on Upwork and I have a contract,

00:29:09.353 --> 00:29:13.802
and it goes really poorly,
like I'm treated poorly.

00:29:13.802 --> 00:29:15.423
What is my recourse?

00:29:15.423 --> 00:29:18.647
How do I deal with that if I have
a really terrible experience,

00:29:18.647 --> 00:29:20.510
I feel like I'm being mistreated?

00:29:21.740 --> 00:29:26.651
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we have our customer
experience team which I have

00:29:26.651 --> 00:29:31.663
the good fortune of getting to
work with quite closely who,

00:29:31.663 --> 00:29:37.094
they're parsed to file a case and
say hey, here are the issues.

00:29:37.094 --> 00:29:41.553
But also because a lot of the work
is done on our platform and

00:29:41.553 --> 00:29:45.831
we have milestones,
we can actually check and see hey,

00:29:45.831 --> 00:29:50.745
you both agreed on this work was
good versus not and allows us to be

00:29:50.745 --> 00:29:56.490
able to arbitrate in a very fair way for
clients and talent alike.

00:29:56.490 --> 00:30:01.476
We have time trackers with variety of
different features that protect both sides

00:30:01.476 --> 00:30:06.462
of the marketplace and something goes
wrong then we're there to help support and

00:30:06.462 --> 00:30:09.180
get to a speedy resolution.

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:13.350
&gt;&gt; And I'm going to go back to,
I think Melissa had a question for you.

00:30:13.350 --> 00:30:17.615
So I'll bring it back and then Melissa
can make sure, keep me honest about it.

00:30:17.615 --> 00:30:21.453
But often we have complex
teams in organizations now and

00:30:21.453 --> 00:30:27.087
that seems like it'd be difficult to put
together in the kind of freelance style or

00:30:27.087 --> 00:30:31.105
the way at least I understand or
I'm envisioning Upwork.

00:30:31.105 --> 00:30:34.289
So on the employer side,
if they're using this model,

00:30:34.289 --> 00:30:38.210
how do they manage those
complex integrated teams?

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:42.205
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I guess there's a couple
different dimensions to that.

00:30:42.205 --> 00:30:45.718
We have enterprise companies
who use our platform and

00:30:45.718 --> 00:30:50.713
they have talent clouds and different
pools of workers who collaborate with

00:30:50.713 --> 00:30:55.475
them to get different needs addressed
depending on the hiring manager,

00:30:55.475 --> 00:30:59.010
the particular needs of
the hiring manager, right?

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:02.691
So if I'm at Acme Co, I might need
a certain type of work done and

00:31:02.691 --> 00:31:07.481
I'll get a set of folks that I'll add to
my virtual talent bench and I'll work with

00:31:07.481 --> 00:31:12.364
them over time and someone can also, who's
in another function do the same thing.

00:31:12.364 --> 00:31:16.595
But then in addition to individual
freelancers, we also have agencies on our

00:31:16.595 --> 00:31:20.700
platform which are effectively sometimes
are completely independent and

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:23.390
in other cases there
are groups of freelancers.

00:31:23.390 --> 00:31:27.298
So sometimes we'll have really successful
freelancers on the platform who decide

00:31:27.298 --> 00:31:30.994
they want to create an agency and have
multiple freelancers who work with them.

00:31:30.994 --> 00:31:36.337
And then clients will engage with that
agency sometimes to hire individual

00:31:36.337 --> 00:31:42.210
freelancers or just source of variety of
freelancers for a more complex project.

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:47.170
And so that goes back to my earlier
point about this being in my view, quite

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:52.610
different from gig work because in some
cases these projects are quite complex,

00:31:52.610 --> 00:31:58.130
they're expensive, they're over a long
time duration, people are working for

00:31:58.130 --> 00:32:03.983
months at a time to be able to I support
the needs of their clients as well but

00:32:03.983 --> 00:32:08.552
I think Professor Valentine is
right to nudge that there will

00:32:08.552 --> 00:32:13.135
probably be there is increasing demand for
talent tonight.

00:32:13.135 --> 00:32:17.518
And not only find each other
within the upper community and

00:32:17.518 --> 00:32:21.990
to not only be able to build
their own agencies, but also for

00:32:21.990 --> 00:32:25.955
potentially other ways to work, to evolve.

00:32:25.955 --> 00:32:31.360
So I think we're paying close attention
to where customers are telling us and

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:35.360
we'll see where things go from there,
&gt;&gt; And my mother said,

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:38.572
you mentioned when you were
talking about gig work,

00:32:38.572 --> 00:32:43.686
that upwork is a little bit different
because they've been profitable for while.

00:32:43.686 --> 00:32:46.987
They've been doing this for a long time,
they have a certain amount of scale.

00:32:46.987 --> 00:32:54.127
But there are other big companies that
are huge who have not been, profitable and

00:32:54.127 --> 00:33:00.743
been dependent upon VC money to keep
prices arguably, artificially low.

00:33:00.743 --> 00:33:04.915
How do you think about
those companies going for?

00:33:04.915 --> 00:33:08.426
What do you expect to happen when those
prices started to go up, as in, for

00:33:08.426 --> 00:33:12.127
example, if Uber and Lyft, it appears
that they're starting to go up a bit?

00:33:12.127 --> 00:33:13.899
Do you think that's viable long term?

00:33:19.468 --> 00:33:24.440
&gt;&gt; So I think the rideshare industry is

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:29.413
also kind of like a specific section

00:33:29.413 --> 00:33:34.231
of the gig economy so with Upwork.

00:33:34.231 --> 00:33:37.729
So I was at when you were asking
the question I wasn't sure if you

00:33:37.729 --> 00:33:41.887
were referring to, I've worked with
companies that are doing things like

00:33:41.887 --> 00:33:45.390
software development or
movie animation or things like that.

00:33:46.420 --> 00:33:50.653
So I don't know that I actually don't
know that I can comment on the kind of

00:33:50.653 --> 00:33:53.200
industry profitability for Uber and Lyft.

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:55.580
I don't know if either of you
have can comment on that.

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:58.070
I don't I know that I can speak to
the ride ride hailing industry.

00:33:59.070 --> 00:34:01.045
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I guess I just wonder,

00:34:01.045 --> 00:34:05.460
is the gig economy in our current
social context, is it viable?

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:09.980
That's kind of what, we don't have to call
it Uber or Lyft, but the gig economy.

00:34:09.980 --> 00:34:12.550
because I would say I'm going to
separate Upwork a little bit right?

00:34:12.550 --> 00:34:15.451
They're not in the same kind of
category as some of the,

00:34:15.451 --> 00:34:18.290
what I think most of us have
direct experience with and

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:21.781
there's a variety of ways that we
engage with gig work a lot of us.

00:34:21.781 --> 00:34:26.699
I'm wondering do you think in
the current social political

00:34:26.699 --> 00:34:31.612
space that the way gig work is
set up is that it's in your mind

00:34:31.612 --> 00:34:36.177
is that viable
&gt;&gt; I see yeah so I think there

00:34:36.177 --> 00:34:43.078
are different trends that are pretending
that this will be like a larger,

00:34:43.078 --> 00:34:48.330
part of the economy like
a larger way of working.

00:34:48.330 --> 00:34:52.824
I think that the gig economy is part of it
the gig economy as we currently understand

00:34:52.824 --> 00:34:57.062
it, where you have startups that
are creating platforms that connected with

00:34:57.062 --> 00:34:59.430
Networks of laborers like labor.

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:01.150
That's part of it.

00:35:01.150 --> 00:35:04.332
But what I was describing before with,
actually,

00:35:04.332 --> 00:35:09.072
I can point to the research by Jerry
Davis, who's a professor at Michigan.

00:35:09.072 --> 00:35:11.788
And his research looks
at the population of

00:35:11.788 --> 00:35:15.880
public corporations in
the United States over time.

00:35:15.880 --> 00:35:22.090
And he's shown that the number of public
corporations in the US is shrinking.

00:35:22.090 --> 00:35:24.075
The number and like the population and

00:35:24.075 --> 00:35:27.420
the size of public
corporations is just smaller.

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:34.610
He gives an example of when Circuit City
in 2009 Circuit City liquidated.

00:35:34.610 --> 00:35:38.175
They had I think it was
like 30,000 employees and

00:35:38.175 --> 00:35:42.205
then someone bought them and
then they became just a website so

00:35:42.205 --> 00:35:47.730
now they're just like a website where
you're fulfilling orders for electronics.

00:35:47.730 --> 00:35:52.058
So instead of having a large set of full
time employees now you have like a website

00:35:52.058 --> 00:35:55.483
The site basically where people
are able to buy electronics and

00:35:55.483 --> 00:35:57.891
what made that possible was an API, right?

00:35:57.891 --> 00:36:01.173
So we have APIs that are basically
connecting different parts of companies.

00:36:01.173 --> 00:36:02.813
You can now have an API to marketing.

00:36:02.813 --> 00:36:05.138
You can have an API to HR and
API to accounting.

00:36:05.138 --> 00:36:09.242
So instead of needing to have a large
corporation that has each of these

00:36:09.242 --> 00:36:10.015
functions.

00:36:10.015 --> 00:36:14.649
You can now sort of have this small
lean staff maybe that connects to

00:36:14.649 --> 00:36:18.895
different functions that you'll
spin up from time to time.

00:36:18.895 --> 00:36:23.740
So I'm hearing a lot of discussion
about how there might be kind of small

00:36:23.740 --> 00:36:25.194
core to accompany and

00:36:25.194 --> 00:36:30.305
then a lot of kind of contingent labor
of some sort in the periphery around.

00:36:30.305 --> 00:36:34.698
So I think we're seeing that that seems
likely it's not a question of whether it's

00:36:34.698 --> 00:36:35.460
profitable.

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:37.820
It's kind of it's what's
happening right now I think.

00:36:37.820 --> 00:36:38.370
&gt;&gt; I see.

00:36:38.370 --> 00:36:40.650
And do you see that as
exacerbating inequality?

00:36:40.650 --> 00:36:44.339
So you have like this core of people
who are in the company in these and

00:36:44.339 --> 00:36:47.381
then the I don't,
I'll call it the periphery around or

00:36:47.381 --> 00:36:49.731
the people who were contract with around.

00:36:49.731 --> 00:36:53.703
Do you imagine like let's say this
is you're out in the workforce,

00:36:53.703 --> 00:36:57.510
do you want to be the contractor or
do you want to be central?

00:36:57.510 --> 00:37:00.620
&gt;&gt; Right exactly,
it's a great [LAUGH] question.

00:37:00.620 --> 00:37:03.770
Yeah, I actually I have
a story about that.

00:37:03.770 --> 00:37:06.865
So my research collaboration team and

00:37:06.865 --> 00:37:11.420
I did a study where we used
Upwork to create flash teams.

00:37:11.420 --> 00:37:14.886
We just had a platform that basically
pulled it together teams from Upwork and

00:37:14.886 --> 00:37:16.390
they work together.

00:37:16.390 --> 00:37:19.500
It was published in the New York Times and
we got a little bit of attention for it.

00:37:19.500 --> 00:37:22.430
And we were sort of engaging
with people on Twitter about it.

00:37:22.430 --> 00:37:26.718
And somebody tagged me on it and
they were like, if this is so great,

00:37:26.718 --> 00:37:30.358
why are you trying to get
tenure at Stanford University?

00:37:30.358 --> 00:37:34.272
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Great question, great question.

00:37:34.272 --> 00:37:36.841
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I just I've told this story

00:37:36.841 --> 00:37:40.290
like, [LAUGH] every day
since that's how I respond.

00:37:40.290 --> 00:37:41.640
It is was such a last question.

00:37:41.640 --> 00:37:43.060
Question, it was really important.

00:37:43.060 --> 00:37:44.990
It's really an important question.

00:37:46.420 --> 00:37:50.549
Yeah, and I think there's a lesson to be
drawn from it because what I love about my

00:37:50.549 --> 00:37:53.952
job is actually everything that
Sam was describing, like I have so

00:37:53.952 --> 00:37:56.660
much flexibility to pick my projects.

00:37:56.660 --> 00:37:59.924
And I have, like guaranteed
pay like lifelong employment,

00:37:59.924 --> 00:38:02.520
I have health insurance I have benefits.

00:38:02.520 --> 00:38:07.020
So I think people who are working in
the gig economy these like we all I guess

00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:12.044
society needs to be careful about all the
language that we use about flexibility.

00:38:12.044 --> 00:38:14.816
When we're speaking from a place where we
have a lot of benefits kind of built in

00:38:14.816 --> 00:38:16.930
around everything that we're doing.

00:38:16.930 --> 00:38:20.020
Otherwise, it is very likely to exacerbate
inequality as you're saying, yeah.

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:24.753
&gt;&gt; And Sam, what do you think are the
limits of the kind of freelance work that

00:38:24.753 --> 00:38:25.660
Upwork supports?

00:38:25.660 --> 00:38:26.488
Right so it's,

00:38:26.488 --> 00:38:30.233
I don't assume that an organization
is going to become a fully freelance.

00:38:30.233 --> 00:38:35.530
I don't even know what that would mean but
fully freelance.

00:38:37.770 --> 00:38:40.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I guess a couple thoughts.

00:38:40.950 --> 00:38:45.297
I think it's probably interesting
to ponder how the gig economy and

00:38:45.297 --> 00:38:48.280
the creator economy
are sort of interfacing.

00:38:48.280 --> 00:38:52.692
And if you think about a lot of
the entrepreneurs on the Upwork platform,

00:38:52.692 --> 00:38:56.091
who are freelancers,
like many of them are creators and

00:38:56.091 --> 00:38:59.225
they see themselves with
a very entrepreneurial.

00:38:59.225 --> 00:39:01.611
Then in terms of delivering products and

00:39:01.611 --> 00:39:06.104
services that then form their portfolios
and then drive additional work and

00:39:06.104 --> 00:39:09.486
higher dollars per hour or
higher fixed project prices.

00:39:09.486 --> 00:39:13.814
I think that's an interesting,
interesting sync point.

00:39:13.814 --> 00:39:18.491
But I'm continuously surprised
by not only the breadth of

00:39:18.491 --> 00:39:21.169
skills that are on the platform.

00:39:21.169 --> 00:39:26.136
But then also how many companies
are realizing areas that

00:39:26.136 --> 00:39:31.102
they thought may not have been
able to support hybrid work

00:39:31.102 --> 00:39:36.601
are support freelancing that they
find ways for that to occur.

00:39:36.601 --> 00:39:40.219
So I expect we're going to see more and
more of that over time now,

00:39:40.219 --> 00:39:42.894
we're primarily focused on knowledge work.

00:39:42.894 --> 00:39:49.126
So I think there are categories where
we are not, you know, present or

00:39:49.126 --> 00:39:54.954
for whatever reason it's we're
not the right solution for but.

00:39:54.954 --> 00:39:59.738
We have people who hire and
get hire paralegals,

00:39:59.738 --> 00:40:03.679
folks who have accounts and we have.

00:40:03.679 --> 00:40:09.261
See customer support,
team members and you pick the arena.

00:40:09.261 --> 00:40:13.520
We have people who provide music
lessons through project catalog.

00:40:13.520 --> 00:40:17.139
So you pick the arena,
knowledge work that can be done,

00:40:17.139 --> 00:40:21.528
virtually or even done virtually
even if the person on the other side

00:40:21.528 --> 00:40:26.310
of the contract is your next door
neighbor and you don't know it.

00:40:26.310 --> 00:40:29.680
Is something that Upwork supports.

00:40:29.680 --> 00:40:36.549
And so I'm going to be really interested
to see how those vectors evolve over time.

00:40:36.549 --> 00:40:42.334
But it's going to be completely driven by
the demand that we see from workers and

00:40:42.334 --> 00:40:46.609
as workers want to have more
flexibility in their lives.

00:40:46.609 --> 00:40:51.459
And don't want to go back into
the office and drive two hours each day

00:40:51.459 --> 00:40:56.151
to laugh at jokes from their boss
they never thought were funny.

00:40:56.151 --> 00:41:00.098
I think you'll see more and
more people choose freelancing and

00:41:00.098 --> 00:41:04.260
categories that we may not
even be aware of right now.

00:41:04.260 --> 00:41:06.520
&gt;&gt; I'm still curious about the limit.

00:41:06.520 --> 00:41:07.915
So let's say I'm a CEO.

00:41:07.915 --> 00:41:12.890
Our company's completely virtual,
everybody's virtual.

00:41:12.890 --> 00:41:15.578
Why don't I need a CTO or
a chief product officer,

00:41:15.578 --> 00:41:18.855
why don't I just get freelance
people doing that with that?

00:41:18.855 --> 00:41:20.317
Is that possible?

00:41:20.317 --> 00:41:21.053
Is that plausible?

00:41:21.053 --> 00:41:23.711
Would that be a good idea
from your perspective?

00:41:23.711 --> 00:41:29.614
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I have heard about companies
who are using fractional executives for

00:41:29.614 --> 00:41:31.050
that very reason.

00:41:31.050 --> 00:41:36.367
Sometimes they will stand up
organizations with a series of C-suite

00:41:36.367 --> 00:41:42.336
leaders who will split their time across
three or four different companies.

00:41:42.336 --> 00:41:47.712
So people are experimenting
with that even today.

00:41:47.712 --> 00:41:51.229
How that will evolve over time and
what the pros and

00:41:51.229 --> 00:41:55.102
cons of that will be,
I'm not sure I can speak to that.

00:41:55.102 --> 00:42:01.065
But I do think that the general trend of
us innovating with work which has had this

00:42:01.065 --> 00:42:08.640
sort of really, I think universally shared
in many respects, set of characteristics.

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:12.583
Now that remote work is
causing us to question things,

00:42:12.583 --> 00:42:18.842
probably makes us really challenge whether
or not some of the assumptions we've had,

00:42:18.842 --> 00:42:23.750
across the board, including that
use case are truly constraints.

00:42:24.980 --> 00:42:26.310
&gt;&gt; May I build on that Brian?

00:42:26.310 --> 00:42:27.110
&gt;&gt; Of course, yes.

00:42:28.280 --> 00:42:32.191
&gt;&gt; It's just another story, so
I've given the flash teams talk to

00:42:32.191 --> 00:42:35.088
executive audiences for
the past five years and

00:42:35.088 --> 00:42:40.200
I think that their question is what
you're asking, what's the limit of this?

00:42:40.200 --> 00:42:44.301
So I'll present and I'll be like, flash
teams you can build this new team and

00:42:44.301 --> 00:42:45.067
it will help.

00:42:45.067 --> 00:42:47.285
And at the start, everyone was like,
wow that's so cool.

00:42:47.285 --> 00:42:50.925
And then increasingly the question
that I get, starting three years ago,

00:42:50.925 --> 00:42:54.793
the question I started to get was always
like, yes, the problem is not access,

00:42:54.793 --> 00:42:55.920
it's integration.

00:42:55.920 --> 00:42:57.660
The problem is always integration.

00:42:57.660 --> 00:43:01.728
I think people are starting to buy into
the idea that you can kind of access a lot

00:43:01.728 --> 00:43:02.981
of talent quickly, but

00:43:02.981 --> 00:43:06.820
integrating what they build is the real
challenge at this point I think.

00:43:06.820 --> 00:43:09.590
&gt;&gt; So you think that is a limit right now?

00:43:09.590 --> 00:43:10.405
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:43:10.405 --> 00:43:12.742
&gt;&gt; So I was wondering if all
this is making me think,

00:43:12.742 --> 00:43:15.763
is the corporate structure we have,
or at least the way we think

00:43:15.763 --> 00:43:19.309
about organizations, is that are you
all suggesting that's outdated?

00:43:19.309 --> 00:43:24.140
Or that we're going to move beyond
that in the foreseeable future?

00:43:24.140 --> 00:43:27.806
I mean, because Sam, that's what I
hear you saying that is a distinct

00:43:27.806 --> 00:43:32.800
possibility and I'm curious Melissa, and
Sam, you can agree or not agree with that.

00:43:32.800 --> 00:43:34.910
I'm curious but Melissa,
what do you think about that?

00:43:35.990 --> 00:43:39.394
&gt;&gt; This is the question that I'm
glad that you posed it like that,

00:43:39.394 --> 00:43:43.776
this is the question that I think about,
and talk about with people all the time.

00:43:43.776 --> 00:43:47.969
And I think we have a classic theory
of the firm, the reason that we have

00:43:47.969 --> 00:43:53.109
organizations is because hierarchy has
just particular coordination properties.

00:43:53.109 --> 00:43:57.130
It's easier to integrate, there's a little
bit easier to have accountability and

00:43:57.130 --> 00:43:59.282
to integrate complex work using hierarchy,

00:43:59.282 --> 00:44:01.621
that's like classic economic theory,
right?

00:44:01.621 --> 00:44:06.528
So I haven't seen anything
that challenges that idea.

00:44:06.528 --> 00:44:13.380
I think this idea of having a core and
periphery is probably where we're headed.

00:44:13.380 --> 00:44:17.472
But having some sort of firm at the center
because it coordinates complex work,

00:44:17.472 --> 00:44:21.670
that's just a structure that
coordinates complex work differently.

00:44:21.670 --> 00:44:25.017
And there's actually a great paper
by some professors at MIT that talk

00:44:25.017 --> 00:44:29.030
about how accountability sort of
accrues within a hierarchy differently.

00:44:29.030 --> 00:44:32.310
So society sort of depends on
people to point to something and

00:44:32.310 --> 00:44:35.320
be like,
that company is accountable for this.

00:44:35.320 --> 00:44:39.090
So accountability and coordination
are sort of served by hierarchy.

00:44:39.090 --> 00:44:41.160
And that's what the firm does.

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:44.787
So that's kind of what I'm thinking
about right now in terms of is how far

00:44:44.787 --> 00:44:46.210
this will go.

00:44:46.210 --> 00:44:49.247
&gt;&gt; Great, and Sam,
you have the last word on that.

00:44:49.247 --> 00:44:55.367
&gt;&gt; Well,
I think that we've already seen how work

00:44:55.367 --> 00:45:01.339
gets done in corporations
change quite a bit.

00:45:01.339 --> 00:45:06.981
Folks who manage by walking around or
who were used to FaceTime or

00:45:06.981 --> 00:45:12.947
what have you, all those things
had to evolve during the pandemic.

00:45:12.947 --> 00:45:16.601
And then people started to adapt the way
that they managed in very different

00:45:16.601 --> 00:45:17.363
ways, right?

00:45:17.363 --> 00:45:21.255
Like weekly Slack updates started too and

00:45:21.255 --> 00:45:26.814
virtual all hands and
virtual happy hours and Zoom calls and

00:45:26.814 --> 00:45:32.167
all sorts of different dynamics
started to come to bear.

00:45:32.167 --> 00:45:35.400
I think that there are parallel tracks
of innovation that are underway.

00:45:35.400 --> 00:45:40.435
I think folks are systematically trying
to build tools that will reinvent and

00:45:40.435 --> 00:45:42.780
reinvigorate the workforce.

00:45:42.780 --> 00:45:46.177
And in parallel you have these
trends of the remote natives and

00:45:46.177 --> 00:45:50.350
remote work underway that I think
are going to challenge these assumptions.

00:45:50.350 --> 00:45:56.140
I do see value for the concept of a firm.

00:45:56.140 --> 00:46:01.230
But I think how that is constructed,
configured and even the work or

00:46:01.230 --> 00:46:06.675
composition of it over time, is we're
going to be experimenting with it,

00:46:06.675 --> 00:46:09.647
I think as a society for a little while.

00:46:09.647 --> 00:46:14.252
And that's incredibly exciting
because this is a frontier

00:46:14.252 --> 00:46:19.223
of innovation that I think there's
probably being less room for

00:46:19.223 --> 00:46:23.009
experimentation that is
now being challenged.

00:46:23.009 --> 00:46:26.328
&gt;&gt; I mean, I agree with both of you
that we're clearly going to see a lot of

00:46:26.328 --> 00:46:27.378
experimentation and

00:46:27.378 --> 00:46:30.484
it's going to be interesting to
see how it all evolves overtime.

00:46:30.484 --> 00:46:36.555
So thank you both very much for
talking with me today about it.

00:46:36.555 --> 00:46:37.360
&gt;&gt; Thanks so much.

00:46:38.660 --> 00:46:42.073
&gt;&gt; All right, so next week I'll discuss
the role of business, philanthropy and

00:46:42.073 --> 00:46:44.330
government to provide
safety to the people.

00:46:44.330 --> 00:46:49.638
We'll talk to Aisha Nyandoro,
CEO of Springboard to Opportunities,

00:46:49.638 --> 00:46:54.760
